😂 Exactly. Fictional bookends.

We just accidentally closed the circuit:

\boxed{\text{Fiction} \rightarrow [\text{constructed boundary}] \rightarrow \text{Function}}

Zero and infinity are perfect examples of the broader move: construct conceptual anchors that let us reason about what lies between or approaches them.

But now generalize it.

A fictional bookend is an intentionally constructed extreme, boundary, character, scenario, or counterfactual used to expose the structure of the real space between possibilities.

F_0\;|\;\text{Reality Space}\;|\;F_1

They don’t have to occur in reality to provide useful coordinates.

“Perfectly rational actor.”

“Frictionless surface.”

“Absolute zero.”

“Infinite population.”

“Utopia.”

“Worst-case scenario.”

“Perfect competition.”

“The ideal hero.”

None needs to exist exactly as represented. Their function can be orientation.

Then you can interpolate:

F_0 \longleftrightarrow x \longleftrightarrow F_1

and ask where reality actually sits.

Even better, deliberately invent different fictional bookends around the same phenomenon and see how the resulting interpretation changes.

[F_A,F_B]\rightarrow P_1

[F_C,F_D]\rightarrow P_2

Then:

\boxed{\Delta P=P_2-P_1}

reveals how much of our “pattern” came from reality versus the coordinate system we constructed around it.

That gives fiction a surprisingly rigorous role:

\boxed{\textbf{Fiction can function as experimental geometry for thought.}}

And stories give us especially elaborate fictional bookends. Jesus as imagined moral observer, Leslie crossing alone, Rocky continuing after being hit—characters and events become reference states against which present behavior can be located.

Not proof.

Not necessarily prediction.

Coordinates.

Which means your earlier line gets another rendering:

We are the embodiment of the stories we encounter.

Stories may partly furnish the fictional coordinate systems through which we locate ourselves.

And then reality supplies the consequence.

